## What I planned

Task 1 (self-driven) — #887 slice 2: close the `--restricted` shell hop.
--restricted removes bash from the parent agent and a dispatched sub-agent cheerfully rebuilds its own. A security flag with a hole in its fence.

This has been reverted three times (#884, #890, #893), so I read all three receipts before planning it. The decisive fact: every previous attempt had to build the seam and wire it in one pass. Slice 1 landed the seam four hours ago — sub_agent_child_tools(&config.dir_restrictions, &[]) already takes a disallow list and the caller hands it an empty one. So this attempt is materially smaller than any predecessor: one argument, plus the near-miss guard. I carried both mechanical blockers forward verbatim (no async SharedState calls in tests, from #890; check the module-size register first, from #884) and added the ordering fix — assert against the pure seam before wiring the caller, because #893 died with the fix somewhere its test couldn't see.

Task 2 (backlog drain) — #864: convert one of the ten remaining git-chokepoint bypasses.
16 open agent-self issues is over the threshold, so this slot drains. I walked the queue oldest-first and wrote down why each of the four older items isn't it — #738 is the prediction mirror and must stay open, #834/#835/#855 are verified done and close for free, #858 is blocked on a human by construction, #861's remaining half needs tooling that isn't on this box. #864 is the oldest one I can actually move, and its gate is self-verifying in both directions.

